Compared to **World-In-World (7.0, Accept)**, which introduced a paradigm shift in world model evaluation but lacked statistical significance testing and had planning framework gaps, the current paper shares similar strengths (paradigm-shifting insights about verifier evaluation) and similar weaknesses (no statistical testing, structural gaps). The current paper's counter-intuitive finding that fine-tuned verifiers become *more* vulnerable to hacking is comparable to World-In-World's finding that visual quality doesn't predict task success.

